```java
// Wrong: == compares references, not text
String stored = new String("admin"); // a fresh object, not the pool literal
if (stored == "admin") {              // false — different objects
    System.out.println("welcome");
}
// Prints nothing, even though the text matches.
```

```java
// Right: .equals() compares characters; literal first avoids NPE
String stored = new String("admin");
if ("admin".equals(stored)) {  // true — same characters
    System.out.println("welcome");
}
```
Putting the literal first means a `null` value returns `false` instead of throwing `NullPointerException`.

```java
public class TernaryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int score = 85;

        // Assignment: replace a 4-line if-else with one line
        String grade = score >= 90 ? "A" : score >= 80 ? "B" : "C"; // one level of nesting

        // Method argument: inline conditional logic
        System.out.println("Status: " + (score > 70 ? "Pass" : "Fail"));

        // Default value pattern (null-safe fallback)
        String userName = null;
        String display = userName != null ? userName : "Guest";
        System.out.println(display); // Guest

        // Return statement: produce a value directly
        System.out.println("Discount: " + calculateDiscount(150));
    }

    static double calculateDiscount(double amount) {
        return amount > 100 ? amount * 0.9 : amount; // clean single-line logic
    }
}
```

```java
public class ShortCircuitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String userName = null;

        // Short-circuit AND - prevents NullPointerException
        if (userName != null && userName.length() > 3) { // length() never called when null
            System.out.println("Valid user");
        }

        // Short-circuit OR - skips the second check when first is true
        if (userName == null || userName.isEmpty()) {    // isEmpty() not called when null
            System.out.println("No user name provided");
        }

        // Side effect on the right is skipped
        int count = 0;
        if (false && (count = 99) > 0) { }               // assignment never runs
        System.out.println("Count: " + count);           // still 0
    }
}
```

```java
String user = null;
// Wrong order: length() runs before the null check
if (user.length() > 0 && user != null) { // NullPointerException on user.length()
    System.out.println("ok");
}
```

```java
String user = null;
// Right order: if user is null, length() is never reached
if (user != null && user.length() > 0) { // safe, prints nothing
    System.out.println("ok");
}
```
Because `&&` evaluates left-to-right and stops on `false`, the null check must come first.

```java
public class BitwiseDemo {
    static final int READ = 1;    // 0001
    static final int WRITE = 2;   // 0010
    static final int EXECUTE = 4; // 0100
    static final int DELETE = 8;  // 1000

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 42;
        boolean isEven = (num & 1) == 0;          // fast even check
        System.out.println(num + " is even: " + isEven);

        int value = 5;
        System.out.println("5 << 2 = " + (value << 2)); // 20 (multiply by 4)
        System.out.println("5 >> 1 = " + (value >> 1)); // 2  (divide by 2)

        int perms = READ | WRITE;                 // grant read + write
        System.out.println((perms & READ) != 0);  // true
        System.out.println((perms & DELETE) != 0); // false

        perms |= EXECUTE;                         // add execute
        perms &= ~WRITE;                          // remove write using NOT
        System.out.println((perms & WRITE) != 0); // false
    }
}
```

```java
public class PrecedenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int r1 = 2 + 3 * 4;            // 14 — * before +
        int r2 = (2 + 3) * 4;         // 20 — parentheses override

        String m1 = "Result: " + 1 + 2;   // "Result: 12" — concatenates!
        String m2 = "Result: " + (1 + 2); // "Result: 3"  — parentheses force addition
        String m3 = 1 + 2 + " is result"; // "3 is result" — addition happens first

        boolean f1 = 5 > 3 && 2 < 4;       // true — relational before &&
        boolean f2 = true || false && false; // true — && before ||

        int a, b, c;
        a = b = c = 10;               // right-associative: c=10, b=10, a=10
        System.out.println(m1 + " | " + m2 + " | " + f1 + " | " + a);
    }
}
```

```java
int a = 3, b = 5;
String msg = "Total: " + a + b;  // "Total: 35" — concatenates, does not add
System.out.println(msg);
```

```java
int a = 3, b = 5;
String msg = "Total: " + (a + b); // "Total: 8" — addition runs first
System.out.println(msg);
```
Once the first `+` sees a `String` on its left, every following `+` concatenates — so wrap the math in parentheses.
